<h1>Records &amp; Result</h1>

<p>
  Kex models data directly. A <code>record</code> is a product type — named,
  typed fields, built with braces:
</p>

<Code code={`record Point do
  x : Float
  y : Float
end

Point { x: 1.0, y: 2.0 }`} repl />

<p>
  A <code>type</code> union describes a value that's one of several shapes.
  Match on it exactly like you matched <code>Just</code>/<code>None</code> in
  the last lesson:
</p>

<Code code={`type Shape = Circle(Float) | Square(Float)

let area(s) = match s do
  Circle(r) -> Math.PI * r * r
  Square(s) -> s * s
end

area(Circle(2.0))`} repl />

<p>
  <strong>Result</strong> is a union built into the prelude for functions that
  can fail: <code>Ok(value)</code> or <code>Error(reason)</code>. Write it as
  <code>T or! E</code> in a signature.
</p>

<Code code={`let safeDiv(a, b) -> Int or! String do
  return Error("div by zero") if b == 0
  return Ok(a / b)
end

safeDiv(10, 2)`} repl />

<Code code={`safeDiv(10, 0)`} repl />

<p>
  Failure is a value, not an exception — <code>match</code> handles both
  cases explicitly, the same honest way you handled <code>None</code>.
</p>
